<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!--
  Logging configuration for this module's tests only.

  The library ships no logging configuration of its own: an application that embeds it picks
  its own binding and its own levels, and a configuration inside a jar would silently become
  everyone else's. Test resources are not packaged, and logback prefers logback-test.xml over
  any logback.xml on the classpath, so this file is only ever seen by the tests here.

  Without it the tests would fall back to logback's built-in default, which is DEBUG on the
  console for every logger - including Netty's and Vert.x's.

  Levels, and what each one is for:

    INFO   the node's own lifecycle - started, stopped, bootstrapping, maintenance.
    WARN   something is wrong with THIS node and someone should look.
    ERROR  something is wrong with this node and it is not working.
    DEBUG  what the node rejected and why - throttled, undecryptable, unparseable, a token that
           did not verify, a message from an address we will not route to.

  A node under test is therefore quiet at INFO. When diagnosing a failure, raise the package
  that rejected the traffic and expect volume proportional to whatever the test is sending:

    <logger name="io.bosonnetwork.kademlia.rpc" level="DEBUG"/>
    <logger name="io.bosonnetwork.kademlia.impl" level="DEBUG"/>

  Console only. A rolling file appender here would write a log into whatever directory the
  test JVM happened to start in.
-->
<configuration>
  <appender name="STDOUT" class="ch.qos.logback.core.ConsoleAppender">
    <encoder>
      <pattern>%d{HH:mm:ss.SSS} [%thread] %-5level %logger{32} - %msg %ex{full}%n</pattern>
    </encoder>
  </appender>

  <logger name="io.netty" level="OFF"/>
  <logger name="io.vertx" level="OFF"/>

  <root level="INFO">
    <appender-ref ref="STDOUT" />
  </root>
</configuration>
